  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Polynomial division and partial fractions break complicated algebraic fractions into simpler pieces.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Reference — Partial fractions",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "A proper fraction \\( \\tfrac{px+q}{(x+a)(x+b)} \\) splits as \\( \\tfrac{A}{x+a}+\\tfrac{B}{x+b} \\). If the fraction is improper, divide first.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Linear factors",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\tfrac{A}{x+a} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Repeated",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\tfrac{A}{x+a}+\\tfrac{B}{(x+a)^{2}} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Improper",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "divide first",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Cover-up",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "find \\( A,B \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 8.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Express \\( \\dfrac{1}{x(x+1)} \\) in partial fractions.</p>\\[ \\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+1}. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example — Improper partial fractions",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Express \\(\\dfrac{x^3+2x}{x^2-1}\\) in partial fractions (after division).</p><p>Degree of numerator ≥ denominator, so divide first: \\(x^3+2x=(x^2-1)\\cdot x+3x\\). So:</p>\\[\\frac{x^3+2x}{x^2-1}=x+\\frac{3x}{(x-1)(x+1)}=x+\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{x+1}.\\]<p>Cover-up: \\(A=\\tfrac{3}{2}\\), \\(B=\\tfrac{3}{2}\\). Result: \\(x+\\dfrac{3/2}{x-1}+\\dfrac{3/2}{x+1}\\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "Express \\(\\dfrac{3x+1}{(x+1)(x-2)}\\) in partial fractions.",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "\\(\\dfrac{A}{x+1}+\\dfrac{B}{x-2}\\). Cover-up: let \\(x=2\\): \\(B=\\tfrac{7}{3}\\). Let \\(x=-1\\): \\(A=\\tfrac{-2}{-3}=\\tfrac{2}{3}\\). Answer: \\(\\dfrac{2/3}{x+1}+\\dfrac{7/3}{x-2}\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "quizWidget": {
    "enabled": true
  }
}
